1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,444
Nineteen September, nineteen seventy-six.

2
00:00:03,444 --> 00:00:09,286
Two Iranian fighter jets are sent up over Tehran to chase a light in the sky.

3
00:00:09,286 --> 00:00:13,535
Both crews say their instruments died as they closed in.

4
00:00:13,535 --> 00:00:16,266
The case is still argued over today.

5
00:00:16,733 --> 00:00:18,900
The pilots were professionals.

6
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:20,562
The radar was military.

7
00:00:20,562 --> 00:00:24,970
The report went to the Pentagon, the CIA and the White House.

8
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:28,800
And still, nobody can say for sure what was up there.

9
00:00:29,266 --> 00:00:31,978
So was this a visitor from another world?

10
00:00:31,978 --> 00:00:37,866
Or a night when a planet, a meteor shower and a tired old jet conspired to look like one?

11
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:42,706
Tonight we run it like the tower did: a log, minute by minute.

12
00:00:42,706 --> 00:00:46,233
Every entry gets a label, and at the end, a score.

13
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:48,809
Facts first, theories labelled.

14
00:00:48,809 --> 00:00:49,966
Tower log opened.

15
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:55,263
Chapter one.

16
00:00:55,263 --> 00:00:56,302
The first call.

17
00:00:56,302 --> 00:01:04,400
Around midnight on the nineteenth of September, the phone rings at the Mehrabad air traffic control centre in Tehran.

18
00:01:04,866 --> 00:01:10,863
Residents in Shemiran, the northern hills of the city, are reporting a strange light.

19
00:01:10,863 --> 00:01:15,166
The duty officer at the tower is a man named Hossein Pirouzi.

20
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:18,873
Callers describe a bright object in the sky.

21
00:01:18,873 --> 00:01:20,935
Not an aircraft, not a star.

22
00:01:20,935 --> 00:01:23,733
Pirouzi walks out to look for himself.

23
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:31,425
He sees a rectangular shape, with flashing lights at each end and a smaller red light circling in the middle.

24
00:01:31,425 --> 00:01:32,220
It is night.

25
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:33,214
It is far away.

26
00:01:33,214 --> 00:01:35,800
And it does not look like an aeroplane.

27
00:01:36,266 --> 00:01:40,441
He tells the Air Force deputy commander, General Youssefi.

28
00:01:40,441 --> 00:01:44,687
The general comes out onto his balcony, and he sees it too.

29
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:47,566
The decision is made: send a fighter up.

30
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:50,059
Not a decision made lightly.

31
00:01:50,059 --> 00:01:59,466
It is the Cold War, the border with the Soviet Union is close, and an unknown object over the capital is a security problem first.

32
00:01:59,933 --> 00:02:01,828
One more fact matters here.

33
00:02:01,828 --> 00:02:07,936
Everything so far is the testimony of people on the ground, at a distance, in the dark.

34
00:02:07,936 --> 00:02:09,199
Keep that in mind.

35
00:02:09,666 --> 00:02:10,627
Entry logged.

36
00:02:10,627 --> 00:02:13,212
Shemiran, about half past midnight.

37
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:16,610
Unknown light, several witnesses, one general.

38
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:20,599
Label: evidence of an observation, not yet of a craft.

39
00:02:21,066 --> 00:02:21,902
Chapter two.

40
00:02:21,902 --> 00:02:22,669
The launch.

41
00:02:22,669 --> 00:02:29,566
At about one thirty in the morning, an F-4 Phantom is scrambled from Shahrokhi air base in Hamadan.

42
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:33,099
At the controls is Lieutenant Yadollah Nazeri.

43
00:02:33,099 --> 00:02:39,300
He takes off, and he can see the object from a staggering distance, about seventy miles away.

44
00:02:39,766 --> 00:02:41,219
He flies toward it.

45
00:02:41,219 --> 00:02:44,967
At twenty-five nautical miles, something happens.

46
00:02:44,967 --> 00:02:46,573
His instruments fail.

47
00:02:46,573 --> 00:02:47,721
His radio dies.

48
00:02:47,721 --> 00:02:49,633
His intercom goes silent.

49
00:02:50,099 --> 00:02:51,354
Nazeri turns back.

50
00:02:51,354 --> 00:02:54,980
As he moves away, the instruments come back to life.

51
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:55,608
He lands.

52
00:02:55,608 --> 00:02:58,466
That is the whole of the first intercept.

53
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:06,691
In a nineteen ninety-four interview, he adds a dramatic detail: the object accelerated past him at Mach two.

54
00:03:06,691 --> 00:03:11,433
That claim came eighteen years later, and we mark it as testimony.

55
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:15,400
The sceptic Philip Klass pointed at something odd.

56
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:17,570
Only the first jet had trouble.

57
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:23,099
And that F-4 had a long history of electrical faults, repaired a month earlier.

58
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:26,506
A faulty jet can explain a dead radio.

59
00:03:26,506 --> 00:03:29,600
It does not explain what he was chasing.

60
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,606
Entry logged.

61
00:03:30,606 --> 00:03:31,766
Label: partial.

62
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:33,208
Chapter three.

63
00:03:33,208 --> 00:03:34,601
The second scramble.

64
00:03:34,601 --> 00:03:37,874
About ten minutes later, a second F-4 launches.

65
00:03:37,874 --> 00:03:40,800
This is the one that made the case famous.

66
00:03:41,266 --> 00:03:46,600
Major Parviz Jafari flies, with Lieutenant Jalal Damirian behind him.

67
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:52,166
The radar picks up a target, twelve o'clock high, at twenty-seven miles.

68
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:56,697
They say the return was as big as a Boeing seven-oh-seven.

69
00:03:56,697 --> 00:03:58,098
A tanker-sized echo.

70
00:03:58,098 --> 00:04:03,633
They close at about a hundred and fifty knots, and then it begins to move away.

71
00:04:04,099 --> 00:04:09,336
At twenty-five miles the object backs off, exactly keeping its distance.

72
00:04:09,336 --> 00:04:15,300
The crew sees flashing lights, red, green, orange and blue, in a strobing pattern.

73
00:04:15,766 --> 00:04:22,166
Then, they say, a smaller bright sphere leaves the object and flies straight toward their jet, like a missile.

74
00:04:22,633 --> 00:04:25,612
Jafari tries to fire a Sidewinder missile.

75
00:04:25,612 --> 00:04:32,066
The moment he reaches for the switch, his weapons panel goes dead, and the radios fail too.

76
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:36,121
He dives, hard, into negative-G, trying to evade.

77
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:42,566
The sphere, they say, follows, turns inside his turn, then returns to the mother object.

78
00:04:43,033 --> 00:04:44,100
One more thing.

79
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:50,572
Another bright object, the crew reports, descends and appears to land gently on the ground.

80
00:04:50,572 --> 00:04:53,203
They mark the location, and fly home.

81
00:04:53,203 --> 00:04:54,128
Entry logged.

82
00:04:54,128 --> 00:04:56,333
Label: evidence, from the crew.

83
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:57,689
Chapter four.

84
00:04:57,689 --> 00:04:58,920
The ground search.

85
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:03,366
At first light, a helicopter flies to the spot the pilots marked.

86
00:05:03,833 --> 00:05:05,053
They find nothing.

87
00:05:05,053 --> 00:05:08,917
No craft, no scorch marks, nothing obvious on the ground.

88
00:05:08,917 --> 00:05:11,899
But the search team does get something else.

89
00:05:12,366 --> 00:05:14,558
A beeping signal on their radio.

90
00:05:14,558 --> 00:05:18,326
A transponder, coming from somewhere near the dry lake.

91
00:05:18,326 --> 00:05:21,066
They follow it, as far as a small house.

92
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:27,871
The people living there say that in the night they heard a loud noise and saw a bright flash of light.

93
00:05:27,871 --> 00:05:30,233
Their account is told, but not tested.

94
00:05:30,699 --> 00:05:33,797
Radiation tests were reportedly considered.

95
00:05:33,797 --> 00:05:37,327
The results, if they exist, were never published.

96
00:05:37,327 --> 00:05:39,199
That is an unverified gap.

97
00:05:39,666 --> 00:05:44,630
The skeptic Brian Dunning has a simple explanation for the beeper.

98
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:51,399
It matches the signal of a beacon from an American C-141 transport, ejected by turbulence.

99
00:05:51,866 --> 00:05:55,004
If so, it was a coincidence, not a landing.

100
00:05:55,004 --> 00:05:56,756
We cannot test it today.

101
00:05:56,756 --> 00:05:57,705
Entry logged.

102
00:05:57,705 --> 00:05:58,800
Label: partial.

103
00:05:59,266 --> 00:06:00,288
Chapter five.

104
00:06:00,288 --> 00:06:01,152
The report.

105
00:06:01,152 --> 00:06:08,933
The incident is written up by an American military attaché in Tehran, and sent to the US Air Force.

106
00:06:09,399 --> 00:06:17,533
It goes to the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the CIA, the Defense Intelligence Agency, the State Department and the White House.

107
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,060
That sounds explosive, until you read why.

108
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:26,453
Sensitive reports from the Middle East were routinely sent to a wide list.

109
00:06:26,453 --> 00:06:29,733
The long distribution is not a sign of alarm.

110
00:06:30,199 --> 00:06:34,472
General Shapour Azarbarzin interviews the crews himself.

111
00:06:34,472 --> 00:06:41,033
In January nineteen seventy-seven, he adds a claim: the object's speed was Mach three.

112
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:42,844
The report is real.

113
00:06:42,844 --> 00:06:46,170
The fighter chase happened as a recorded event.

114
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:50,699
What the report cannot do is prove what the pilots were chasing.

115
00:06:51,166 --> 00:06:52,133
Entry logged.

116
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:57,266
Label: evidence the case was taken seriously, not evidence of aliens.

117
00:06:57,733 --> 00:06:58,564
Chapter six.

118
00:06:58,564 --> 00:06:59,465
The sceptics.

119
00:06:59,465 --> 00:07:01,821
Philip Klass offers a bold answer.

120
00:07:01,821 --> 00:07:06,533
The bright light that began it all, he says, was the planet Jupiter.

121
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,968
In September nineteen seventy-six, Jupiter was bright, and in the right part of the sky.

122
00:07:12,968 --> 00:07:18,733
Witnesses at a distance, with no reference points, can easily mistake it for a craft.

123
00:07:19,199 --> 00:07:21,443
Dunning adds a second ingredient.

124
00:07:21,443 --> 00:07:29,600
The nineteenth of September falls in the peak of three meteor showers, including the Southern Piscids and Eta Draconids.

125
00:07:30,066 --> 00:07:37,075
Klass also suggests the radar lock may have been in a manual mode, which can cause false returns.

126
00:07:37,075 --> 00:07:41,266
A mistaken lock explains a target that keeps its distance.

127
00:07:41,733 --> 00:07:47,841
So a mix: a bright planet, a few meteors, a jet with electrical faults, and crews on edge.

128
00:07:47,841 --> 00:07:49,198
It is a tidy answer.

129
00:07:49,198 --> 00:07:51,166
But does it cover everything?

130
00:07:51,633 --> 00:07:52,773
Here is the gap.

131
00:07:52,773 --> 00:07:59,397
A planet cannot explain two separate crews, two different jets, and a second set of failures.

132
00:07:59,397 --> 00:08:03,600
Sceptics reply that only the first jet really lost systems.

133
00:08:04,066 --> 00:08:09,107
The truth is that the second crew's account rests on their word alone.

134
00:08:09,107 --> 00:08:10,043
Entry logged.

135
00:08:10,043 --> 00:08:13,500
Label: unverified, with a plausible explanation.

136
00:08:13,966 --> 00:08:14,931
Chapter seven.

137
00:08:14,931 --> 00:08:16,034
The long shadow.

138
00:08:16,034 --> 00:08:20,033
Why does this case still hold on after almost fifty years?

139
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,609
Because it was a military case.

140
00:08:22,609 --> 00:08:26,488
Trained pilots, a general, radar, and an official report.

141
00:08:26,488 --> 00:08:31,933
It is the case people cite when they say, serious people have seen these things.

142
00:08:32,399 --> 00:08:35,071
And the pilots never changed their story.

143
00:08:35,071 --> 00:08:36,504
They were not hoaxers.

144
00:08:36,504 --> 00:08:42,433
It is likely they described what they truly experienced, in a frightening, confusing night.

145
00:08:42,899 --> 00:08:44,261
Which is the lesson.

146
00:08:44,261 --> 00:08:48,346
A sincere witness is not the same as an accurate instrument.

147
00:08:48,346 --> 00:08:52,566
And an official report proves only that someone wrote it down.

148
00:08:53,033 --> 00:08:58,645
The records to settle it, the radar tapes and maintenance logs, were never made public.

149
00:08:58,645 --> 00:09:02,000
Without them, this case cannot be closed either way.

150
00:09:02,466 --> 00:09:05,233
Time to close the tower log, and score the night.

151
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:10,068
A bright light seen over Tehran by several witnesses: evidence.

152
00:09:10,068 --> 00:09:14,367
Two F-4s scrambled and a radar lock: evidence, from the crews.

153
00:09:14,367 --> 00:09:19,290
Instrument failures: partial, since the first jet had a faulty history.

154
00:09:19,290 --> 00:09:23,866
The helicopter search finding nothing: evidence against a landing.

155
00:09:24,333 --> 00:09:29,878
The beeping transponder: debunked, a likely American C-141 beacon.

156
00:09:29,878 --> 00:09:33,407
Jupiter and meteors as the cause: partial.

157
00:09:33,407 --> 00:09:36,599
A Mach three alien craft: no evidence.

158
00:09:37,066 --> 00:09:37,887
The verdict.

159
00:09:37,887 --> 00:09:39,734
Unexplained, but not alien.

160
00:09:39,734 --> 00:09:50,133
Something truly strange happened, at least to the people involved, but a craft that outflew a Phantom is a claim without a recording, a wreck or a test.

161
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:53,383
And that is what makes it so good a story.

162
00:09:53,383 --> 00:09:58,817
Men who trusted their gauges watched them fail, and they never stopped asking why.

163
00:09:58,817 --> 00:10:01,799
That deserves respect, and it deserves proof.

164
00:10:02,266 --> 00:10:09,041
Next case: a glowing object that fell into the water off Nova Scotia in nineteen sixty-seven.

165
00:10:09,041 --> 00:10:13,266
Reports from fishermen, police, and the Canadian military.

166
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:16,277
What do you think the pilots were chasing?

167
00:10:16,277 --> 00:10:21,366
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
